{"type":"message","id":"d599c911","parentId":"a3cf1c61","timestamp":"2026-05-27T08:14:58.170Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The actual fragment syntax used by the workspace is `{{> name }}` (Handlebars), not `{>name<}` (parser). The parser uses `{>name<}` syntax. These are different!\n\nLooking at the compile.rs: it uses `{{> fragment-name }}` regex. The parser uses `{>name<}` as seen in the lexer (`FragmentOpen` is `{>` and `FragmentClose` is `<}`).\n\nSo the parser's fragment syntax `{>name<}` is different from the compile/runtime `{{> name }}` syntax. The goal says to \"replace the `FRAGMENT_INCLUDE_RE` regex with the parser to detect fragment inclusions.\"\n\n\nBut if we use the parser for fragment detection, we'd be detecting `{>name<}` instead of `{{> name }}`. This seems like a planned migration from old `{{> }}` to new `{> <}` syntax. Looking at the tests in lint and check modules, they're using `{{> note }}` - those would break if we switch unused_fragment.rs to use the parser. Actually, re-reading the step plan more carefully: \"For fragment detection in `unused_fragment.rs`, parse each source file and collect `Node::Fragment` values.\" The parser's fragment nodes come from `{>name<}` syntax, not `{{> name }}`.","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_016UHQG3jEYvTDF8WWPn1Nt9","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd X:/repo/skillet/.worktrees/goal-move-the-lint-pipelines-p-mpnrlu69-qmv6ab && cargo test --locked 2>&1 | tail -30","timeout":120}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"github-copilot","model":"claude-sonnet-4.6","usage":{"input":1,"output":494,"cacheRead":30407,"cacheWrite":858,"totalTokens":31760,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779869688342,"responseId":"msg_bdrk_01M8AK83gmva7zupiqGAJR4Q"}}
